#
# Round 58: metis.ko (a Xiaomi CPU-scheduling/game-boost driver) crashes with
# a NULL pointer dereference in lowlt_list_del_task the moment real
# second-stage boot ramps up scheduling activity (right as logd starts) -
# confirmed via a captured kernel oops in /sys/fs/pstore/console-ramoops-0
# (Round 57's writeup in README has the full trace).
#
# Round 57 tried removing metis.ko/mi_schedule.ko/task_turbo.ko from
# modules.load directly - confirmed NOT to work: this device's
# init.insmod.mt6789.cfg drives loading via `modprobe -a` (not raw
# sequential insmod), and modprobe auto-loads a module's hard dependencies
# from modules.dep regardless of whether that dependency's own line is
# present in modules.load. scheduler.ko/cpufreq_sugov_ext.ko/mtk_core_ctl.ko
# (kept, matching the sibling yunluo tree) all hard-depend on metis per
# modules.dep, so metis kept getting pulled back in as a dependency even
# with its own line removed - confirmed by two byte-identical
# console-ramoops captures showing the exact same crash after the "fix".
#
# The actual, working mechanism: system/core/libmodprobe's blocklist_ (see
# Modprobe::IsBlocklisted) is consulted *during* dependency resolution too
# ("Hard-dependencies cannot be blocklisted" -> the *dependent's* load
# fails cleanly instead of silently pulling the blocklisted module back in).
# modules.load has been restored to its stock content (metis.ko/
# mi_schedule.ko/task_turbo.ko all present again, matching yunluo... no,
# matching this device's own stock list) - blocking happens here instead,
# which is the properly-supported, reversible mechanism for this exact
# case. rootdir/bin/init.insmod.sh already handles the "-b" prefix
# (`"-b *" | "-b") arg="-b $(cat modules.load)"`), and
# vendor/etc/init.insmod.mt6789.cfg has a blob_fixup (extract-files.py)
# changing `modprobe|*` to `modprobe|-b *` to actually pass it.
#
# Blast radius (unavoidable - these all hard-depend on metis/mi_schedule
# per modules.dep, and "hard-dependencies cannot be blocklisted" fails
# their own load too, not just skips metis): scheduler, cpufreq_sugov_ext,
# mtk_core_ctl, task_turbo, vip_engine, mtk_fpsgo_v3, fpsgo,
# powerhal_cpu_ctrl, ccidvfs, mtk_perf_common, perf_common_v, and
# mtk-vcodec-sys-api. NOTE (Round 69): that last one does NOT cost hardware
# video. mtk-vcodec-sys-api is a 12 KB shim whose only metis-tainted edge is
# vip_engine; the real drivers - mtk-vcodec-dec (500 KB) and mtk-vcodec-enc
# (477 KB) - depend on mtk-vcodec-common/iommu/heap/smi/slbc/thermal only,
# never on metis, and load normally. HW decode and encode both work; what is
# lost is the codec's VIP-scheduler hook (thread-priority/latency tuning).
# Deleting the metis/mi_schedule files outright would have the identical
# blast radius; blocklist is kept instead because it's reversible without
# a re-extract if a newer HyperOS dump ever ships a fixed metis.ko.
blocklist metis
blocklist mi_schedule
# task_turbo.ko already hard-depends on metis (modules.dep), so it would
# fail via the dependency chain anyway - blocklisted directly too, purely
# to skip straight past it instead of a noisy failed insmod attempt+log
# line on every boot.
blocklist task_turbo
